                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "8946194",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198000879653",
                            "num_reviews": 161,
                            "playtime_forever": 172,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 0,
                            "playtime_at_review": 172,
                            "last_played": 1391329630
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This game is not worth buying.\n\nYou're placed into an admittedly good-looking environment and given a simple goal (getting to the moon in as many different ways possible) and a few simple abilities to facilitate this (a double jump and a dash button, both acquired within the first five minutes). You'll probably even enjoy finding the first two or three paths to the moon.\n\nHowever, after perhaps half an hour of playing, the game becomes a tedious log through overly-large labyrinthian environments designed to pad out the game as long as possible. And if you think you're finally done once you get all six endings, you're dead wrong--once you do, a secret [i]seventh[/i] ending is unlocked, requiring you to redo the platforming for [i]all six[/i] of the previous endings again, except this time you're stuck in a thunderstorm. Once you've done that, you're [i]still[/i] not done; a new part of the map is unlocked, requiring you to beat several [i]more[/i] obnoxiously hard jumping puzzles...[b]which cannot be completed[/b] due to a persistent bug with the game's cannon physics that has been reported by dozens of users and the developers have stubbornly refused to fix.\n\nThe game starts out decent, but then alternates between mindlessly dull and bugged into impossibility.",
